---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Terminal from '../../components/Terminal.astro';
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Coordination Primitives"
  seoTitle="Multi-Agent Coding Coordination with ostk"
  description="Coordinate AI coding agents through project state, file generations, CAS edits, VFS projections, durable queues, and explicit conflict handling."
  sectionLabel="REFERENCE // COORDINATION"
>
  <p class="font-headline text-lg text-on-surface-variant italic leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-12">
    Coordination operates without message buses, active servers, or RPC. ostk projects state directly into the filesystem, managing concurrency via per-file generation checks, resolving collisions with mechanical merges, and routing interrupts using durable queues.
  </p>

  <!-- Section 1: Virtual Filesystem (VFS) Overlay -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <div class="section-label mb-4">01 // VIRTUAL_FILESYSTEM</div>
    <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">VFS: The Observation Substrate</h2>
    
    <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      The VFS is a FUSE filesystem owned by the daemon. It projects the daemon's live memory and registries as standard directories. Any tool (e.g., <code class="text-primary">ls</code>, <code class="text-primary">cat</code>, <code class="text-primary">grep</code>) can query kernel state directly without needing JSON-RPC parsers.
    </p>

    <!-- Mount Commands -->
    <h3 class="section-label-secondary mb-3">VFS_CLI_REFERENCE</h3>
    <Terminal>
      <pre class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary"><span class="text-outline"># Mount at the default path (.ostk/vfs/)</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs mount

<span class="text-outline"># Check mount status & owning daemon PID</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs status
declared:
  auto_mount:  true
  mount_point: ~/.ostk/vfs
actual:
  mounted:     true
  owner:       daemon
  owner_pid:   27060

<span class="text-outline"># Cleanly unmount VFS overlay</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs unmount

<span class="text-outline"># Clean up orphan FUSE mounts left by hard crashes</span>
<span class="text-outline">$ </span>ostk vfs unmount-stale</pre>
    </Terminal>

    <!-- Namespaces -->
    <h3 class="serif-text text-2xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-3 mt-8">Directory Namespaces</h3>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-4 max-w-xl">
      Top-level namespaces map to distinct registries in the daemon memory:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-5 max-w-3xl border border-surface-variant mb-8">
      {[
        { ns: 'needles/', desc: 'Active work items and tickets. Subdirectories expose individual field values.' },
        { ns: 'decisions/', desc: 'Historical decision logs, structured by slug. Documents justifications and timestamps.' },
        { ns: 'fleet/', desc: 'Live agent registry enumerating active, stale, and dead agents.' },
        { ns: 'proc/', desc: 'Detailed process table for active running workers.' },
        { ns: 'journal/', desc: 'Append-only audit logs. Read to observe all kernel transactions.' },
        { ns: 'sys/', desc: 'Kernel-internal caching states and graph index metrics.' },
        { ns: 'mem/', desc: 'Working memory: context pages and capability pin boundaries.' },
        { ns: 'drivers/', desc: 'Active device drivers and their declared capabilities.' },
      ].map(n => (
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-2 text-xs font-body py-2 border-b border-surface-variant/30 last:border-b-0">
          <code class="text-primary md:col-span-2 font-mono">{n.ns}</code>
          <span class="text-secondary md:col-span-10">{n.desc}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <!-- Projection Lattice -->
    <h3 class="serif-text text-2xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-3">The Projection Lattice</h3>
    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-4 max-w-xl">
      To balance performance and token economy, the VFS exposes data at three distinct resolution layers:
    </p>
    <div class="space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">ATOM // 1–30 tokens</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-2">A single property field. Ideal for tight agent polling loops and quick inline context.</p>
        <Terminal>
          <pre class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary"><span class="text-outline">$ </span>cat .ostk/vfs/needles/1338/title
EPIC: Observation substrate over VFS namespace</pre>
        </Terminal>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">OBJECT // One complete entity</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-2">The complete JSON serialization of a single record.</p>
        <Terminal>
          <pre class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary"><span class="text-outline">$ </span>cat .ostk/vfs/needles/1338/_object
{"{"}"id":1338,"title":"EPIC: Observation substrate...","priority":"P0","status":"open"{"}"}</pre>
        </Terminal>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">NAMESPACE // Collection enumeration</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary mb-2 font-mono">Enables directory walks, file searches, and piping.</p>
        <Terminal>
          <pre class="font-mono text-xs text-secondary"><span class="text-outline">$ </span>ls .ostk/vfs/needles/ | wc -l
1413</pre>
        </Terminal>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Optimistic Concurrency Control -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <div class="section-label mb-4">02 // OPTIMISTIC_CONCURRENCY</div>
    <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC)</h2>
    
    <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      Rather than locking files on open (which stalls parallel workflows), ostk uses monotonic generation counters and Compare-and-Swap (CAS) edits.
    </p>

    <img src="/diagrams/write-path.svg" alt="ostk CAS write path from agent request through generation check, merge, audit, and file update" width="620" height="440" loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="w-full max-w-2xl border border-surface-variant mb-8" />

    <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-6 border-l-2 border-primary max-w-3xl">
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <div>
          <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">MONOTONIC GENERATIONS</div>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Every file's generation is tracked in <code class="text-primary">.ostk/gen_table.jsonl</code>. Successful writes increment the generation counter, providing an instant state signature.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">SERIALIZATION LOCKS</div>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            A system-level <code class="text-primary">flock(2)</code> on <code class="text-primary">.ostk/gen_table.lock</code> wraps the generation lookup and modification loop. Concurrent writes are queued, avoiding lost updates.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">SHADOW BACKUPS</div>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            On every generation bump, the pre-edit content is backed up to <code class="text-primary">.ostk/shadows/&lt;path_hash&gt;/gen_&lt;N&gt;</code>. This historic record is used to resolve conflicts if a subsequent write fails.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">CAS OPERATION</div>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
            Write tools (like <code class="text-primary">file:edit</code>) pass the old content string. If the workspace version has changed, the write is blocked, triggering Hot PR conflict resolution.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Hot PR Conflict Resolution -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <div class="section-label mb-4">03 // HOT_PR_RESOLUTION</div>
    <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">Hot PR: Automated Merge Routing</h2>
    
    <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      When a write fails due to a mismatched generation signature, Hot PR attempts to merge the changes mechanically, without invoking expensive LLM rounds.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-px bg-surface-variant max-w-3xl border border-surface-variant">
      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">TIER 1 // AUTO_MERGE</div>
        <div class="font-body text-[10px] text-outline mb-2">Distance &gt; 3 lines</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Edits occur in different, non-overlapping parts of the file. The kernel merges both changes automatically and silently.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">TIER 2 // ASSISTED_MERGE</div>
        <div class="font-body text-[10px] text-outline mb-2">&lt; 30 lines / 2 regions</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Minor overlaps. The kernel computes a line rebase and returns a suggested edit call back to the agent for confirmation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-1 font-bold">TIER 3 // MANUAL_REBASE</div>
        <div class="font-body text-[10px] text-outline mb-2">Over Tier 2 thresholds</div>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Large conflict. The write is rejected. The agent must re-read the file and manually re-apply its logic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: Digest Envelopes & 304 Elision -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <div class="section-label mb-4">04 // DIGEST_AND_ELISION</div>
    <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">Peripheral Aware Digest & 304 Elision</h2>
    
    <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      Instead of querying files repeatedly, the agent checks the 5-line envelope appended to tool outputs. If a file is read unnecessarily, the kernel returns a 304 header.
    </p>

    <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-6 border-l-2 border-primary max-w-3xl mb-6">
      <div class="font-label text-[10px] text-primary mb-3 font-bold">ENVELOPE SECTIONS</div>
      <div class="space-y-2">
        {[
          { tag: '[procs]', desc: 'Active peer agents and execution health. Enables cross-agent status checks.' },
          { tag: '[presence]', desc: 'State glyph tracking arrivals and departures.' },
          { tag: '[files]', desc: 'Modified file paths, generation tokens, and editing agent aliases. Unmodified files are omitted.' },
          { tag: '[loadavg]', desc: 'Active tickets, P0 count, and pending messages.' },
          { tag: '[meminfo]', desc: 'Token usage metrics and remaining context budget.' },
        ].map(item => (
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-12 gap-2 text-xs font-body py-2 border-b border-surface-variant/30 last:border-b-0">
            <code class="text-primary md:col-span-2 font-mono">{item.tag}</code>
            <span class="text-secondary md:col-span-10">{item.desc}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary max-w-xl">
      If an agent attempts to read an unmodified file (e.g. because it is missing from the <code class="text-primary">[files]</code> block), the kernel intercepts the operation and returns <code class="text-primary">[304] path:gen=N (current)</code>. This avoids loading hundreds of lines of duplicate data into the context window.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Nudges -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <div class="section-label mb-4">05 // DURABLE_NUDGES</div>
    <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">Nudges: Cross-Boundary Interrupts</h2>
    
    <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      Nudges are durable, asynchronous messages pushed into an agent's next tool response context, bypassing the need for real-time IPC connections.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-4 max-w-3xl mb-8">
      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <h4 class="font-body text-xs font-bold text-primary mb-1">Local Nudges</h4>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Pushed into the local queue at <code class="text-primary">.ostk/nudges/&lt;agent-alias&gt;.jsonl</code>. The dispatch loop reads this queue on every step and appends pending messages into the target's next turn.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <h4 class="font-body text-xs font-bold text-primary mb-1">Cross-Host Nudges</h4>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Sent using the Files API, writing a <code class="text-primary">nudge.uploaded</code> event to the audit trail. Peer daemons scan the audit log, download the files, and insert them into their local queues.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-surface-container p-5">
        <h4 class="font-body text-xs font-bold text-primary mb-1">Temporal Resiliency</h4>
        <p class="font-body text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Nudges are persistent on disk. If the daemon is restarted, the queue remains intact, ensuring that messages are not lost during kernel upgrades.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 6: Caveats -->
  <section class="mb-16">
    <div class="section-label mb-4">06 // SYSTEM_CAVEATS</div>
    <h2 class="serif-text text-3xl font-medium text-on-surface mb-4">FUSE & VFS Operational Constraints</h2>
    
    <p class="font-body text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      Because VFS relies on the host operating system's FUSE layers, operators should be aware of the following execution limits:
    </p>

    <div class="bg-surface-container-low p-6 border-l-2 border-error max-w-3xl">
      {[
        { issue: 'Feature Flag', body: 'Compiling from source requires the `--features fuse` cargo flag. Standard pre-built binaries include FUSE support by default.' },
        { issue: 'Host Drivers', body: 'macOS requires the macFUSE kernel extension. Linux systems require `libfuse3` installed.' },
        { issue: 'Stale Mounts', body: 'Hard daemon shutdowns (SIGKILL or OOM) leave stale mount references. Run `ostk vfs unmount-stale` to clear lock directories.' },
        { issue: 'Read-Only Projection', body: 'VFS paths are read-only views. State modifications (e.g. creating needles) must be done through CLI tools or JSON-RPC verbs.' },
      ].map(c => (
        <div class="flex gap-6 text-xs font-body py-3 border-b border-surface-variant/30 last:border-b-0">
          <span class="text-on-surface w-48 shrink-0 font-bold">{c.issue}</span>
          <span class="text-secondary leading-relaxed">{c.body}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="metadata-ribbon border-t border-surface-variant">
    <span class="font-label text-[10px] text-secondary mr-4">RELATED:</span>
    <a href="/docs/agent-lifecycle/"><Icon name="sync" size="14" class="align-middle mr-1" />AGENT_LIFECYCLE</a>
    <a href="/docs/context-management/"><Icon name="layers" size="14" class="align-middle mr-1" />CONTEXT_MGMT</a>
    <a href="/docs/architecture/"><Icon name="architecture" size="14" class="align-middle mr-1" />ARCHITECTURE</a>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
